# Event Branding

Customize the branding for individual events, or let them inherit your workspace defaults. Event branding primarily affects how your RSVP pages appear to guests.

## Inheritance from Workspace

By default, events inherit all branding from your workspace:

- Logo (light and dark versions)
- Primary and accent colors
- Dark mode color variants

This means you can set up branding once at the workspace level and have it apply to all your events automatically.

## Accessing Event Branding

To customize branding for a specific event:

1. Navigate to your event
2. Click the **Branding** tab

You'll see your current branding settings, whether inherited or custom.

## Using Workspace Branding

The **Use workspace branding** toggle controls inheritance:

- **ON** - Event uses workspace branding (default)
- **OFF** - Event has custom branding

When inheritance is on, you'll see the inherited values displayed with an "Inherited" badge.

## Customizing Event Branding

To create custom branding for an event:

1. Turn **OFF** the "Use workspace branding" toggle
2. The form expands to show customization options
3. Upload logos and set colors as desired
4. Click **Save Changes**

### Event-Specific Logo

Upload a different logo for this event:

- Useful for co-branded events
- Event-specific visual identity
- Partner logos

### Event-Specific Colors

Set different colors for this event:

- Match a sponsor's brand
- Create event-specific themes
- Differentiate from other events

### Background Image

Add a background image to your RSVP pages:

1. Upload an image or enter a URL
2. Select a **Display Mode**:
   - **Cover** - Image covers the entire background, cropping if needed
   - **Contain** - Entire image is visible, may show gaps
   - **Repeat** - Image tiles to fill the space
   - **Center** - Image centered without scaling

{% callout type="info" %}
Background images work best with subtle, low-contrast images that don't distract from form content.
{% /callout %}

### Card Accent Settings

Add a colored accent bar to cards on RSVP pages:

- **Enable Card Accent** - Toggle to show/hide the accent bar
- **Accent Color** - Choose the color (defaults to primary color)
- **Position** - Top, Bottom, Left, or Right edge
- **Thickness** - Thin, Medium, or Thick

### Section Header Settings

Customize section headers on RSVP forms:

- **Background Color** - Set a background color for headers
- **Text Color** - Set the text color for headers

## Resetting to Workspace Defaults

To remove custom branding and return to workspace defaults:

1. Turn **ON** the "Use workspace branding" toggle
2. Confirm the reset
3. All custom branding is cleared

{% callout type="warning" %}
Resetting removes all custom event branding. This cannot be undone, but you can reconfigure custom branding again if needed.
{% /callout %}

## RSVP Page Appearance

Event branding affects how guests see your RSVP pages:

### Logo

Your event logo (or workspace logo) appears in the header of the RSVP page.

### Colors

- **Primary color** - RSVP submission buttons, confirm/decline actions
- **Accent color** - Links and secondary elements

### Preview

The branding preview shows a mockup of how the RSVP page will appear. Toggle between light and dark mode to see both versions.

## When to Use Custom Branding

### Keep Workspace Branding

- Single organization, consistent brand
- Internal events
- Series of similar events

### Use Custom Branding

- **Co-branded events** - Partner logos and colors
- **Themed events** - Special visual identity
- **Client events** - Customer-specific branding
- **Seasonal events** - Holiday or themed colors

## Best Practices

### Maintain Readability

Ensure your colors provide good contrast:

- Buttons should be readable
- Links should stand out from regular text
- Logos should be clear against the background

### Test with Sample RSVP

Preview your branding and consider testing with a sample guest to see the full RSVP experience.

### Plan Ahead

Set up event branding before sending invitations so guests have a consistent experience from their first interaction.
